course: dictionary
lesson: 14097
title: Learning words by heart
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To memorise something means to learn it so well that you can remember it exactly, without looking. Another way to say it is to learn it by heart.
explain: «Sam had to memorise the nine times table before the test on Friday.» On the day, he could not look at a card. He had to know each answer in his head.
explain: «The actors memorised their lines for the school play.» Their lines are the words each actor says on stage, so they had to remember them without reading from a script. Memorising is about remembering the exact words, not just the big idea.
explain: You can memorise a poem, a prayer or a Bible verse too. Good ways to memorise are to say it out loud, to repeat it again and again, and to practise a little every day.
fact: To memorise means to learn something so well that you can remember it exactly.
fact: «Memorise» is a verb: I memorise, he memorises, she memorised, they are memorising.
fact: In American English the spelling is «memorize», with a z.
fact: You can memorise times tables, spellings, a poem, or lines in a play.
fact: Repeating something little and often helps you memorise it.
exercise: «Mia wants to memorise her spellings for Friday.» What does memorise mean here? | Copy them onto a poster for the wall | Read them once and throw the list away | *Learn them so well she can write them without looking
exercise: Ben must memorise a poem for assembly. What must he be able to do on the day? | *Say the whole poem from memory, without the book | Find the poem in a book | Draw a picture about the poem | Count how many lines it has
exercise: Which phrase means the same as memorise? | *Learn by heart | Look up | Hand in | Put away
test: «Priya had to memorise her lines for the play.» What are lines in a play? | The seats in the hall | *The words each actor says on stage | Tickets sold at the door | The music played at the start
test: Which is the best way to memorise a prayer? | Write it down, then throw the paper away | Watch TV while it plays in the background | *Say it aloud again and again, little and often | Read it once and leave it for a year
exam: «Tom can write all ten of his spellings without looking.» What does this show? | He has not tried yet | *He has memorised them | He has copied them from a friend | He has forgotten them
